DRILL FINDING (step 4, worker-71a60ba7): the live leg opened a ZERO-AUTHORITY FAIL-OPEN. Found by a mutation that came back GREEN, fixed in this row, now pinned by a new arm.

THE MUTATION THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN RED. Step 4 mutation (4) says: make `zeroAuthority` map LIVE legs too (read `receipt` off a live entry) -> the MIXED arm goes red or throws. It did NEITHER — `Test Files 1 passed (1)` / `Tests 11 passed (11)`.

WHY IT COULD NOT BE RED, AND WHAT THAT EXPOSED. A LIVE `ClosureLeg` has no `receipt` field, so mapping it stores `nodeRef -> undefined`. `Map.get` cannot tell a key bound to `undefined` from a missing key, so the FOUNDATION filter at goal-qualification.ts:121 is purely TYPE-driven — it is what makes `entry.receipt` well typed and it has ZERO behavioural effect. Chasing why led to the actual defect one line down:

    const receipt = byNode.get(account.nodeKey);
    if (receipt === undefined) continue;      // <- a live node lands HERE

A durable activation naming an approved LIVE node hit that `continue` and was SILENTLY SKIPPED. The goal then closed while an unaccounted lease + effect were still on the ledger — precisely the authority design 278 asks `zeroAuthorityWitness` to prove gone. The in-code comment claimed the opposite ("an activation that names one is unaccounted and refuses below"); it did not refuse, it continued. Comment and code disagreed and the code won.

THIS ROW OPENED IT. Under Foundation-only qualification the state was unreachable: a receipt-less node refused GOAL_CLOSE_VERIFICATION_RECEIPT_ABSENT at goal-qualification.ts:213-216 long before `zeroAuthority` ever ran. The live leg is what makes a receipt-less approved node reach the activation check, so the live leg is what must answer for it.

FIX (goal-qualification.ts, inside `zeroAuthority`, no new refusal code, no new *_LAYER):
    const liveNodes = new Set(bound.filter((e) => e.leg === "LIVE").map((e) => e.nodeRef));
    ...
    if (liveNodes.has(account.nodeKey)) {
      return refuse(GOAL_CLOSE_AUTHORITY_REMAINS,
        "a durable activation names a node proved on the live leg");
    }
Placed BEFORE the `byNode.get` miss. GOAL_CLOSE_AUTHORITY_REMAINS is already in the roster and already raised by three other guards there; per the roster's own convention the MESSAGE distinguishes this one. goal-qualification.ts 202 -> 212 lines (cap 250).

NEW ARM, production-backed, no planted bytes: goal-live-evidence.test.ts "refuses AUTHORITY_REMAINS when an activation names a live node". The world is built with `seedProvenAttempt(store, ACTIVATION_WORLD_NODE_KEY, [...])` — the FIRST HALF of `seedVerifiedNode` (goal-closure-test-fixtures.ts:534, already exported), which runs the shipped Foundation dispatch service and writes the activation but NOT the verification receipt. Add `seedReviewAcceptance` + a COMMITTED `seedLandingReceipt` and the node is otherwise fully closable — the arm asserts that first via `readLiveNodeEvidence(...) toMatchObject({landing:"COMMITTED", leg:"LIVE"})`, so the refusal cannot be mistaken for the landing rule or the acceptance rule firing. Then `expectRefusedExactly(outcome, "GOAL_CLOSE_AUTHORITY_REMAINS", "a durable activation names a node proved on the live leg")` — code AND layer DAEMON_PREREQUISITE AND message — plus `expectUnmoved`. `it.runIf(win32)` for the same reason the other two Foundation-touching arms are (the attempt service pins the Claude runtime to an absolute Windows path).

DRILL 4b, the re-aim: delete the four-line guard -> RED, "refuses AUTHORITY_REMAINS when an activation names a live node", `Tests 1 failed | 11 passed (12)`. Restored, SHA256 5e302263393ac5f87fa7d1de73b027811e9e41f36e98689aba31e24858aad09f.

SECOND GREEN MUTATION, recorded, NOT a defect. Step 4 mutation (3) — replace the leg tag with a constant — also came back green. The tag is redundant against the rest of the preimage: FOUNDATION contributes one part (`receipt.receiptSha256`), LIVE three (`verifierReceiptSha256`, `landing`, `landingReceiptId`), and `derivedRef` is length-framed, so no fixture can collide on the tag alone. The arm's real subject is leg-blindness, so the drill was re-aimed: making `acceptanceParts` fully leg-blind produced an EXACT collision — `expected '2104fac1...' not to be '2104fac1...'` — which is the replay hazard the arm exists to catch. The tag stays (defence in depth, and it is what keeps the preimage self-describing), now honestly recorded as unpinned-by-itself rather than falsely credited.
